%I A146747 #13 Oct 27 2026 23:45:01
%S A146747 2,3,5,6,20,30
%N A146747 Numbers k such that sigma_1(k)*sigma_0(k)/(sigma_1(k)-sigma_0(k)) is an integer.
%C A146747 Numbers k such that A000203(k)*A000005(k)/(A000203(k)-A000005(k)) is an integer.
%C A146747 This sequence is finite and complete. Proof: write s = sigma_1(k), d = sigma_0(k). For k = 1, s = d and the quotient is undefined. For k >= 2, s > k >= d, and since s*d = d*(s-d) + d^2, the quotient is an integer iff (s-d) | d^2, so k < s <= d^2 + d. Writing sigma_0(k)/k^(1/3) as the product over prime powers p^a || k of (a+1)/p^(a/3), each factor is at most 1 for p >= 11 and is maximized at 2^3, 3^2, 5, 7, so sigma_0(k) <= C*k^(1/3) with C = 48/2520^(1/3) = 3.527... (equality at k = 2520). Hence k < C^2*k^(2/3) + C*k^(1/3), which fails for k >= 2058, and a direct search of 2 <= k < 2058 gives exactly the terms listed. - _Jennifer Voitle_, Oct 20 2026
%t A146747 q[n_] := Module[{s = DivisorSigma[1, n], d = DivisorSigma[0, n]}, Divisible[s*d, s - d]]; Select[Range[2, 100], q] (* _Amiram Eldar_, Apr 07 2024 *)
%o A146747 (PARI) is(n) = {my(f = factor(n), s = sigma(f), d = numdiv(f)); n > 1 && !((s*d) % (s-d));} \\ _Amiram Eldar_, Apr 07 2024
